In <199406281455.AA16208@panix.com>, "Alan G. Isaac" writes:
> I have to side with Netsurfer against Ray in this debate. One of
> the classic failures of libertarian theory [a tipoff that the
poster is about to digress from libertarian theory -- cP]



 is its inattention to
> children. Specifically, to what extent should we view children
> as rights bearers? If children have rights against their parents,
> as I believe any reasonable libertarian vision must allow, then
> a serious enforcement problem arises.

Procreation is a choice, also, the consequences of which rest with the
parents, up to some agreed-upon [question-begging alert] age of
majority or other criterion of separate competence. There are many
terrible slippery slopes involved in any claim that a child is
entitled to some minimal degree of nurture from its parents; poorer
parents necessarily cannot provide as much of any good, education
included, as wealthy parents; however, there are legions of productive
and well-educated people who got that way despite poor beginnings and
despite the best efforts of public schools to stunt them, because they
got,early on, from their parents or elsewhere, a love of learning.


 After all, they are still
> _children_. The idea that a child could borrow against its future
> earnings in private markets to ensure, despite the lack of interest
> of its parents, that it acquires an adequate education is absurd,
> even if we make the risible assumption that children are informed
> and foresightful enough to understand what is at issue.

People are desperate to share what they know, and what they are
interested in. Look at the 'Net, for Bacon's sake. Anyone who can't
get educated would probably starve in a cornfield.

> Higher education is a separate issue of course. But even here we
> need to grapple seriously with the implications of studies that
> show socioeconomic status is a better predictor of educational
> attainment than ability (as measured by standardized tests and
> grades).

Ah, the sociological fallacy. Do such results say anything
about causality? *no*

It does say that poorer-but-smarter children miss out on some
opportunities, *duh*. But in the other direction, don't forget that
the higher "socioeconomic" [my second-to-least favorite term; my
least fave is being called a "consumer" in any context except the
strictly economic] parents got that way via better education.

laisser faire, laisser passer; le monde va de lui-meme.

