From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Sun, 3 Jul 94 15:55:26 WET DST
Subject: [#94-7-8] Educational Policy

  Don't forget that children already "borrow" against their future
in the current situation. For the "privilege" of attending public
schools, they are expected to make a "contribution" in the future in
the form of taxes to pay for the next generation. Why is this any
less immoral than signing a contract with a public school (or having
your parents do it)? Your parents already sign away your future by voting
on taxes and voting in bureaucrats who decide which public
school you must attend. In both scenarios, it is not the child who
is in control, it is the parents or the state. The decisions the parents
or State make could affect the child for the rest of its life.


  I don't see how this is a condemnation of libertarianism anymore
than any other system. Frankly, I view the act of conceiving a child
as a big decision. Perhaps the child will be born severely handicapped,
or lead miserable life. The child had no say in his creation,
or the environment he must grow up in. Maybe the only time
it is moral to have a child is when the parents are of high socioeconomic
standing. I imagine for the people who want to reduce population growth,
this is nice, but somehow I don't see them supporting the supremacy of
rich people to have children (because of their usual anti-rich stance)
Think about it though. Poor people usually have more children,
their children die sooner, and their children tend to have more children.

(not to say I support any laws for this, but it is sopmething to consider.
Would you like to grow up in Somalia?)

-Ray





