From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Mon, 4 Jul 1994 19:54:53 -0400
Subject: [#94-7-11] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears? 

 sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu  (Gregory Sullivan) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-6-197:
>My conclusions from recent history are different.  During the Cuban missile
>crisis there was an unprecedented amount of uncertainty. We were close to
>nuclear armageddon.  The amount of damage the human species might have
>inflicted upon itself was also unprecedented.  This uncertainty was due to
>technological innovations, especially missiles, and nuclear bombs.  I do not
>claim that uncertainty in military interactions will simplistically
>monotonically increase over time.  I do think the overall modern trend is up.

 There was much less uncertainty about what each side was doing in the Cuban
missile crisis than in previous conflicts. It is possible that there was more
uncertainty about the intentions of the people involved, although it may just
appear that way because the decision making process was more openly and
widely reported.
 I do see a factor which tends to counteract the effects of better information
gathering. Most military leaders try to plan their strategies looking at
military history, so that they can learn from mistakes of others rather than
from trial and error. Accelerating technological change is probably causing
each war to be less similar to preceding wars, thereby increasing the
uncertainty about what strategies should be chosen.
